From 36faa0d1735450a38873f025eae69be50e95e9cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 17:39:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(sandbox): copy skills on Windows through pinned,
 reparse-refusing handles

Windows has no O_NOFOLLOW/dir_fd opens, so the anchored copy was withheld there and
every Docker skills mount came up empty (regression vs. the path-based copy).

tools/credential_files_windows.py walks the tree through CreateFileW handles opened
with FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT and without FILE_SHARE_DELETE: every directory from
the drive root down, and each file while it is read, stays open so it cannot be renamed
or replaced mid-read. Each handle must be non-reparse, of the expected type, match the
listed (st_dev, st_ino), and have GetFinalPathNameByHandleW equal to the expected path.
A root that cannot be pinned yields nothing; a platform with neither mechanism still
gets an empty copy (fail closed), never a path-following one.

Tests: race tests run through both the POSIX walk and a Linux emulation of the handle
API (reparse/identity/final-path checks; emulation cannot model share modes). Real
Win32 tests (junction swaps, renames refused while pinned, clean-tree copy, read-only
refresh) are platforms("windows") and have not run natively: the fork's tests-os lane
is disabled.
